PLACES=[
N('west-point-foundry-cold-spring','West Point Foundry Preserve','Cold Spring, Town of Philipstown','midh','industry','ruins',41.41535,-73.94685,'site','public','walk','','1817–1911',
  'The West Point Foundry was set up at Cold Spring after the War of 1812, encouraged by President Madison, who wanted American foundries able to cast artillery; it used Margaret\'s Brook for water power, local iron ore and charcoal, and the river to ship its work. It made engines, pipes and machinery as well as guns, and in 1860 its superintendent Robert Parker Parrott designed the rifled Parrott gun that the Union army used through the Civil War. Cast iron gave way to steel, the works was sold in 1897 to the Cornell brothers, makers of sugar mills, and it closed in 1911. Scenic Hudson owns 87 acres round the site as a preserve; Michigan Tech dug it as an archaeology site from 2002 to 2008.',
  'Only the foundry\'s office building of 1865 stands whole; the rest is ruins (Wikipedia, Scenic Hudson). Scenic Hudson stabilised the office and laid out trails: the half-mile Yellow Foundry Trail follows the old rail line that linked the buildings past the ruins in the order the guns were made, and the one-mile Red Trail goes upstream past the boring mill and its water wheel to more foundry ruins. The pin is the office building. Interior not checked.',
  'The ironworks that made the Parrott gun, in a ravine a short walk from a train station.',
  'Scenic Hudson\'s West Point Foundry Preserve, 68 Kemble Avenue, Cold Spring. Open all year, dawn to dusk; park only in the preserve\'s lot, and commuter parking is not allowed; stay on marked trails; dogs on a leash of 6 feet or less (Scenic Hudson\'s preserve brochure, read November 22, 2026). The Cold Spring station of Metro-North\'s Hudson Line is a short walk away.',
  [W('West Point Foundry'),L('Scenic Hudson: West Point Foundry Preserve brochure','https://www.scenichudson.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/West-Point-ParkBrochures-WPFP-18.pdf')],
  th=[41.41659,-73.95293],thn='Preserve entrance, 68 Kemble Avenue',brick='?',hours='Every day, dawn to dusk'),
N('old-st-peters-van-cortlandtville','Old St. Peter\'s Church and Van Cortlandtville Cemetery','Van Cortlandtville, Town of Cortlandt','midh','worship','standing',41.3129,-73.90115,'site','open','drive','cemeteries','1766 or 1767',
  'A small wooden Episcopal church, 28 by 36 feet, built before the Revolution for the parish that is now St. Peter\'s in Peekskill: Wikipedia and the Town of Cortlandt give 1766, and the parish itself says 1767. During the war it served Rochambeau\'s French troops as a hospital, and Washington is said to have read Morning Prayer there while staying at the Van Cortlandt Upper Manor House nearby. It was restored in 1964. The churchyard holds the graves of 8 French and 44 American soldiers with memorial markers, and of John Paulding, one of the three militiamen who captured Major John André, under a marble monument New York City put up in 1827; General Seth Pomeroy, who died at Peekskill in 1777, lies there in an unmarked grave.',
  'A clapboarded colonial church in its old cemetery on Locust Avenue at Oregon Road, with the Paulding monument and the soldiers\' markers among the graves. Interior not checked.',
  'A 1760s country church where Rochambeau\'s soldiers were nursed and are buried.',
  'Owned by the parish of St. Peter\'s Episcopal Church, Peekskill, and kept with the help of the Friends of Old St. Peter\'s; the parish holds services in it on Memorial Day and the first Sunday in July (St. Peter\'s history page, read November 22, 2026). The cemetery is beside the road; park considerately on Locust Avenue. The Van Cortlandtville Historical Society\'s schoolhouse is next door.',
  [W('Old St. Peter\'s Church (Van Cortlandtville, New York)'),L('St. Peter\'s Episcopal Church, Peekskill: our history','https://stpeterspeekskill.org/our-history'),
   L('Town of Cortlandt: Where History Lives, part 3','https://www.townofcortlandtny.gov/cn/webpage.cfm?tpid=19291')],
  brick='no',hours='Services on Memorial Day and the first Sunday in July'),
N('van-cortlandtville-schoolhouse','Van Cortlandtville School (the Little Red Schoolhouse)','Van Cortlandtville, Town of Cortlandt','midh','civic','standing',41.3125,-73.90028,'site','ask','drive','','c. 1850',
  'Common School District No. 10, a one-room school built about 1850 in a plain Gothic Revival style, with a steep slate roof; additions came in the early 1900s and in 1940. It taught District 10 until 1935, first boys and then boys and girls from kindergarten to eighth grade, and was a kindergarten again from 1952 to 1972. The Van Cortlandtville Historical Society has had it since 1972 and has run an early school days program there for children since 1975. It was listed on the National Register in 1989.',
  'The small schoolhouse at the north end of Locust Avenue, beside Old St. Peter\'s Church and cemetery. Wikipedia calls it brick with board-and-batten siding; the colour was not checked. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Van Cortlandtville Historical Society. The society meets at the schoolhouse on the third Saturday of each month and holds open houses; groups that want to visit leave a message at (914) 736-7868 (the society\'s page, read November 22, 2026). Otherwise view from Locust Avenue.',
  [W('Van Cortlandtville School'),L('Van Cortlandtville Historical Society: about us','https://vancort.net/about-us')],
  brick='?'),
N('van-cortlandt-upper-manor-house','Van Cortlandt Upper Manor House','Van Cortlandtville, Town of Cortlandt','midh','landmark','standing',41.31271,-73.90535,'site','view','drive','','c. 1773',
  'Gertrude Van Cortlandt Beekman inherited the Peekskill part of Van Cortlandt Manor in the 1720s, and a smaller house probably stood here by 1756; her nephew Pierre Van Cortlandt rebuilt or remodelled it in 1773. During the Revolution it sheltered the Van Cortlandt family while their manor house at Croton was threatened and ransacked, and Washington made it his headquarters in November 1776, November 1779 and from June 25 to July 2, 1781. Pierre Van Cortlandt Jr. lived here until 1848; from 1889 it was a convalescent home and later a nursing home. It was listed on the National Register in 1981.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey, five-bay brick house altered by the family in the 1830s, with a slate gable roof and a stuccoed frame wing of the 1920s (Wikipedia). Its present use was not checked. Interior not checked.',
  'Washington\'s headquarters three times over, a few hundred metres from the church he is said to have read prayers in.',
  'Private; its present owner and use were not checked. Look from the public road only.',
  [W('Van Cortlandt Upper Manor House')],
  brick='?'),
N('van-cortlandt-manor-croton','Van Cortlandt Manor','Croton-on-Hudson','midh','landmark','standing',41.19167,-73.87639,'site','view','drive','','1697 grant; house 1700s',
  'Stephanus Van Cortlandt, the first native-born mayor of New York City, was granted the 86,000-acre Manor of Cortlandt by royal charter in 1697, after buying the land from the Kitchawank people and others. This stone and brick house near where the Croton meets the Hudson was the family\'s seat; the British threatened and ransacked it in the Revolution. It is a National Historic Landmark and a museum of Historic Hudson Valley, which tells the story of the "New Nation" years after the war, including the enslaved Black people who lived and worked there.',
  'The colonial manor house in its grounds at 525 South Riverside Avenue, by the Croton–Harmon station. Interior not checked.',
  'A colonial manor house that once ruled 86,000 acres of Westchester.',
  'Historic Hudson Valley. As read November 22, 2026, the house is closed for tours while paths, services and the landscape are restored (sidewalks to the Metro-North station among the work); the grounds are used for The Great Jack O\'Lantern Blaze in the autumn. Check Historic Hudson Valley\'s page before going.',
  [W('Van Cortlandt Manor'),L('Historic Hudson Valley: Van Cortlandt Manor','https://hudsonvalley.org/historic-sites/van-cortlandt-manor/')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('new-croton-dam','New Croton Dam and Croton Gorge Park','Town of Cortlandt','midh','water','standing',41.22639,-73.85575,'site','public','walk','','1892–1906',
  'New York City built the New Croton Dam from 1892 to 1906, to Alphonse Fteley\'s design, across the Croton River below the Old Croton Dam of 1837 to 1842, which was left in place and drowned by the new reservoir. The masonry dam is 297 feet high from base to crest and 266 feet broad at its base, and when finished it was the tallest dam in the world; the reservoir it made covered farms, buildings and six cemeteries. Westchester County\'s Croton Gorge Park, 97 acres at its foot, opened in 1964.',
  'The whole face of the dam and its stepped stone spillway, seen from the park below; paths climb the sides to the road over the top, which crosses a bridge over the spillway that has been reopened to walkers after repairs (Westchester County Parks). The Old Croton Trail along the 1842 aqueduct begins in the park.',
  'A hand-cut stone dam that was the tallest in the world in 1906.',
  'The dam is part of New York City\'s water supply; the park at its foot is Westchester County\'s, at 350 Yorktown Road (Route 129), Croton-on-Hudson. Open every day, 8 a.m. to dusk. No park pass needed; a parking fee ($5 with the county Park Pass, $10 without, cash only) is charged on weekends in May and September and daily in summer (Westchester County Parks, read November 22, 2026).',
  [W('New Croton Dam'),W('Croton Gorge Park'),WC('Croton Gorge Park','https://parks.westchestergov.com/croton-gorge-park')],
  th=[41.22489,-73.85833],thn='Croton Gorge Park lot',brick='no',hours='Every day, 8 a.m. to dusk'),
N('croton-north-station','Croton North station','Croton-on-Hudson','midh','rail','standing',41.20694,-73.89444,'site','view','drive','stations','late 1800s',
  'A New York Central commuter station of the late 19th century, designed by the railroad\'s own engineers in a Queen Anne manner, with terra-cotta trim in its brick walls and big wooden brackets under the roof. It had long been out of service when it was made into offices in 1984, and it was listed on the National Register in 1987 as an excellent example of a commuter station of its time. Two Pullman electric passenger coaches of the 1930s were put on a short new spur north of it in 1986 and are part of the listing.',
  'The brick station with its long overhanging roof and canopies on Senasqua Road, off US 9, beside the Hudson Line and CSX\'s yard; the two old Pullman coaches stand on the cut-off track to the north. Wikipedia says it is used as offices. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Offices on Senasqua Road; the listed parcel belongs to Metro-North. View from Senasqua Road. The tracks beside it are in use: keep off them.',
  [W('Croton North station')],
  brick='?'),
N('underhill-wine-cellars-croton-point','Underhill wine cellars, Croton Point','Croton-on-Hudson','midh','industry','ruins',41.17478,-73.89652,'area','public','walk','','1800s',
  'Robert Underhill bought Croton Point in 1804; his son Richard bred a grape that resisted disease and made the Croton Point vineyards famous for their wine, and his son William made bricks here in two big brickyards that employed several hundred men. The wine was kept in vaulted cellars dug into the hillside. After Richard Underhill died in 1871, the wine left in the vaults was sold off because some of his heirs held strong temperance views (crotonhistory.org, citing an 1878 advertisement). The point is now Westchester County\'s Croton Point Park.',
  'The county says the vaults are still intact, built into the slope on the east side, about midway down the southern part of the point; its historic-features map lists the wine cellar, a wine cottage, the foundation of a fruit barn and the Underhill and Croton brickyards. The pin is an estimate of that part of the point and not the vault itself, which was not found on the aerial. Look at the cellars from outside only.',
  'Wine vaults dug into a hillside for a vineyard whose wine was famous in the 1800s.',
  'Westchester County\'s Croton Point Park, at the end of Croton Point Avenue, off US 9 at the Croton–Harmon station. Open every day, 8 a.m. to dusk; no park pass needed; parking fee ($5 with Park Pass, $10 without) on weekends in May and September and daily in summer (Westchester County Parks, read November 22, 2026). The park road towards the cellars is closed to cars at times; walk it from the lots.',
  [W('Croton Point Park'),WC('Croton Point Park history','https://parks.westchestergov.com/croton-point-park/croton-point-park-history'),
   WC('Croton Point Park','https://parks.westchestergov.com/croton-point-park'),
   L('Croton historian: Ruins of the Underhill wine cellars','https://crotonhistory.org/2013/07/29/ruins-of-the-underhill-wine-cellars/')],
  th=[41.18693,-73.88651],thn='Croton Point Park lot',brick='?',hours='Every day, 8 a.m. to dusk',conf='mixed'),
N('bear-mountain-bridge','Bear Mountain Bridge','Town of Cortlandt','midh','landmark','standing',41.32,-73.98028,'site','public','walk','','1924',
  'A suspension bridge across the Hudson between Bear Mountain and Anthony\'s Nose in Cortlandt, first planned when a bridge company was chartered in 1868; when it opened in 1924 it was the longest suspension bridge in the world, until the Benjamin Franklin Bridge passed it 19 months later. Only the main span is hung from the cables. The New York State Bridge Authority took it over in 1940, and since 1970 the toll has been taken eastbound only.',
  'The steel towers and cables over the river, with sidewalks on both sides that carry the Appalachian Trail across the Hudson; walkers and cyclists may cross. The 1924 Bear Mountain Bridge Road on the Cortlandt side, built as a private toll road with the bridge, is also on the National Register.',
  'The world\'s longest suspension bridge for a year and a half, and the Appalachian Trail\'s way over the Hudson.',
  'New York State Bridge Authority; US 6 and 202. Toll eastbound only. The sidewalks are open to walkers. Parking is in Bear Mountain State Park and at Fort Montgomery on the west side; on the Cortlandt side a small lot on Bear Mountain Bridge Road is mapped but not on public land.',
  [W('Bear Mountain Bridge'),W('Bear Mountain Bridge Road')],
  th=[41.32405,-73.98768],thn='Fort Montgomery State Historic Site lot',brick='no'),
N('yorktown-heights-station','Yorktown Heights station','Yorktown Heights','midh','rail','standing',41.27153,-73.77972,'exact','view','drive','stations','1877',
  'Built in 1877 by the New York, Westchester and Putnam Railway on a line first laid by the New York and Boston Railroad, later the Putnam Division, with a branch from here to Mohansic State Hospital. It is one of three such stations left in Westchester County, and the only property in the town on the National Register (1981). It was restored for use as a museum and Chamber of Commerce office, and a federal grant paid for restoring the whole building inside and out, finished in 2020.',
  'A small one-storey wooden station in Railroad Park on Commerce Street at Underhill Avenue: board and batten below, clapboard above, deep bracketed eaves and a slate roof, with a brick chimney at the back. The old rail bed beside it is the North County Trailway. Interior not checked; its opening times were not found.',
  '',
  'In the town\'s Railroad Park, Commerce Street at Underhill Avenue; view from the park and the North County Trailway.',
  [W('Yorktown Heights station')],
  brick='some'),
N('amawalk-friends-meeting-house','Amawalk Friends Meeting House','Yorktown Heights','midh','worship','standing',41.29259,-73.77152,'site','open','drive','cemeteries','1830s',
  'Quakers met in north central Westchester from the mid-1700s; fire destroyed their first two meeting houses, and this timber-frame one was built in the 1830s by the Hicksite side of the split in American Quakerism, a rare survivor of that kind. A porch was added later in the 1800s. It was listed on the National Register in 1989 with its cemetery, where the war photographer Robert Capa and his brother Cornell are buried, though neither was a Quaker.',
  'A plain meeting house on Quaker Church Road with Greek Revival touches; inside, many original furnishings remain: elders\' benches in three fixed rising steps, loose and fixed benches facing the centre, small tables for the minutes and two wood stoves, one of the late 1800s (Wikipedia). The graves in the cemetery keep to Quaker plainness.',
  'A Hicksite Quaker meeting house of the 1830s, and Robert Capa\'s grave.',
  'Amawalk Monthly Meeting, 2467 Quaker Church Road, Yorktown Heights. The meeting welcomes all to worship on Sundays (Amawalk Friends Meeting\'s page, read November 22, 2026).',
  [W('Amawalk Friends Meeting House'),L('Amawalk Friends Meeting','https://amawalkquakers.org')],
  brick='?',hours='Meeting for worship Sunday 10:30 to 11:30 a.m.'),
N('sylvan-glen-mohegan-quarries','Mohegan granite quarries, Sylvan Glen Park Preserve','Lake Mohegan, Town of Yorktown','midh','industry','ruins',41.29521,-73.84749,'site','public','hike','','to about 1941',
  'Granite was quarried on this hillside for building, and the Trail Conference says it supplied a honey-coloured granite for the Cathedral of St. John the Divine in New York City. The quarries were the Town of Yorktown\'s largest employer of their day and stopped working at about the beginning of the Second World War. The town bought part of the land in 1981 and opened it as a park when more was added.',
  'Quarry pits of several sizes, old roads, old cables, cast-off granite slabs, the remains of buildings and a shed said to have stored explosives, in the western part of the preserve, with interpretive signs near the workings (Town of Yorktown, Trail Conference). The High Quarry Trail (blue, 0.8 mile) and the short orange Quarry Drive and Quarry Oak trails reach them. The pin is the quarry ruins as mapped in OpenStreetMap; the quarry faces are steep.',
  'The hillside quarry that cut stone for St. John the Divine, left to the woods.',
  'Town of Yorktown\'s Sylvan Glen Park Preserve; the west entrance and lot are at the end of Morris Lane, Lake Mohegan, and there is an east lot as well. Hours not checked: the town\'s page gives none.',
  [L('Town of Yorktown: Sylvan Glen Park Preserve','https://www.yorktownny.gov/recreation-community/parks-preserves-trailways/town-parks-preserves/sylvan-glen-park-preserve/'),
   L('New York-New Jersey Trail Conference: Sylvan Glen Park Preserve','https://secure.nynjtc.org/node/2524')],
  th=[41.29964,-73.85256],thn='Sylvan Glen lot at the end of Morris Lane',brick='no'),
N('lincoln-depot-peekskill','Peekskill Freight Depot (Lincoln Depot Museum)','Peekskill','midh','rail','standing',41.2877,-73.92982,'exact','open','drive','stations','late 1800s',
  'Abraham Lincoln stopped at Peekskill on February 19, 1861, on his train journey from Springfield to his inauguration, and spoke to the crowd here, his only public appearance in Westchester County. The brick freight depot that stands on the spot was built later in the 1800s and is the only intact freight-only station left on the old Hudson River Railroad. It was listed on the National Register in 2004 and opened as the Lincoln Depot Museum in 2014.',
  'A one-storey brick freight house, 96 by 30 feet, with a shallow gabled roof whose eave reaches 8 feet out over the raised loading platform on brackets, decorated bargeboards at the gable ends, and pilasters and corbelling in the brickwork (Wikipedia). Before it became a museum it kept its cement floor, wainscoting and open roof trusses; the inside now holds the museum, not checked.',
  'Where Lincoln spoke on his way to be sworn in.',
  'Lincoln Depot Museum, South Water Street, Peekskill, by the Metro-North tracks. The museum\'s page says it is open from April to November 23 in 2026 but gives no days or times (read November 22, 2026); check its page before going.',
  [W('Peekskill Freight Depot'),L('Lincoln Depot Museum','https://www.lincolndepotmuseum.org/')],
  brick='?',hours='April to late November; days not given on the museum\'s page'),
N('st-peters-episcopal-peekskill','St. Peter\'s Episcopal Church, Peekskill','Peekskill','midh','worship','standing',41.29222,-73.91917,'exact','open','drive','','1892',
  'The parish began before the Revolution at Old St. Peter\'s in Van Cortlandtville, one of 14 parishes in the Episcopal Diocese of New York older than the war. This stone church, its third building on this ground, was designed by Richard M. Upjohn in the manner of the English country churches his father built, and dates from the late 19th century; the Register list gives 1892. Hobart Upjohn, Richard\'s son, designed the interior renovations of 1926. It was listed on the National Register in 2003 with its church hall, lychgate, church house and parish house.',
  'A coursed granite nave with a slate roof and parapeted west gable at Howard and North Division streets; inside, 12 triple-lancet stained-glass windows and a rose window behind the altar (Wikipedia).',
  '',
  'St. Peter\'s Episcopal Church, Howard and North Division streets, Peekskill. Services on Sunday mornings, with one service in summer (the parish\'s own page, read November 22, 2026).',
  [W('St. Peter\'s Episcopal Church (Peekskill, New York)'),L('St. Peter\'s Episcopal Church, Peekskill','https://www.stpeterspeekskill.org/')],
  brick='no',hours='Sunday 8 and 10 a.m.; summer, one service at 9 a.m.'),
N('peekskill-presbyterian-church','Peekskill Presbyterian Church','Peekskill','midh','worship','standing',41.28861,-73.92417,'exact','view','drive','','1846',
  'The congregation dates from 1799 and was formally organised in 1826 by 16 members, 14 of them women; it was among the first churches to elect women as elders. It built this Greek Revival church in 1846, twice the size of its first, and has rung the same bell since the 1790s. The gallery was lowered and an organ added in 1892, the church merged with Peekskill\'s Second Presbyterian in 1920, and a new organ came in 1971. It was listed on the National Register in 2002.',
  'A white clapboard church on a stone foundation, set above South Street behind a retaining wall, with Doric corner pilasters, a pedimented gable and a four-stage bell tower with a clock. Inside: a high flat ceiling, original wainscoting, 36-over-36 windows with their eared surrounds and louvred interior shutters, and columns in the choir loft (Wikipedia). Its service times were not found on a page of its own.',
  '',
  'South and Washington streets, Peekskill, opposite the post office and below the old Drum Hill High School. View from South Street.',
  [W('Peekskill Presbyterian Church')],
  brick='no'),
N('double-arch-ossining','Croton Aqueduct Double Arch, Ossining','Ossining','midh','water','standing',41.16282,-73.86325,'site','public','walk','','1839',
  'The Old Croton Aqueduct, opened in 1842 to bring Croton River water to New York City, crossed the gorge of the Sing Sing Kill at Sing Sing (now Ossining) on a stone arch spanning 88 feet, which a local paper called "stupendous" in May 1839. A wooden bridge for local traffic was soon built under it; today a second, lower arch stands under the first, the "bridge within a bridge" (when it replaced the wooden bridge was not checked). A weir chamber beside it, added 16 years after the aqueduct opened, let keepers shut off the flow and empty the line into the Kill.',
  'The two stacked arches over the gorge, best seen from the viewing platform and the Sing Sing Kill Greenway, an accessible path under the bridges. A plaque on the bridge names Andrew Young, builder. The aqueduct promenade crosses the top. The Ossining weir chamber is opened now and then for guided tours by the state park and the Friends of the Old Croton Aqueduct.',
  'A stone aqueduct arch of 1839 with a road arch built in under it.',
  'Old Croton Aqueduct State Historic Park (New York State Parks), across from the Joseph Caputo Community Center, 95 Broadway, Ossining. The Sing Sing Kill Greenway starts nearby. Village lots on Leonard Street and Central Avenue. Weir tours are on the state park\'s events page.',
  [W('Croton Aqueduct'),L('New York State Parks: Old Croton Aqueduct State Historic Park','https://www.parks.ny.gov/parks/oldcrotonaqueduct/details.aspx'),
   L('Friends of the Old Croton Aqueduct: Ossining arched bridge','https://aqueduct.org/empire-city/ossining-arched-bridge/'),
   L('Croton historian: Celebrating Ossining\'s Double Arch','https://crotonhistory.org/2013/07/20/celebrating-ossinings-double-arch/')],
  th=[41.16202,-73.86287],thn='Leonard Street lot',brick='?'),
]
